2025-08-02T20-44-14-961Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.4235","issue":"2025-08-02T20:44:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KFGZ","awipsid":"AFDFGZ"}]
=================================================



597

FXUS65 KFGZ 022044

AFDFGZ



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Flagstaff AZ

144 PM MST Sat Aug 2 2025



.SYNOPSIS...Hot and dry conditions will continue this weekend and

into much of next week. Slight chances for afternoon showers and

thunderstorms look to return near the middle of next week over the

higher terrain.



&&



.DISCUSSION...Today...A strong ridge of high pressure currently 

centered over southern Arizona begins to build today. The 18Z FGZ 

RAOB shows much drier air, along with a subtle subsidence inversion

aloft. As a result, little to no precipitation is expected across

northern Arizona this afternoon. The only areas that look to see 

any chance of showers, will mainly be in the vicinity of the White

Mountains where some residual moisture remains. Gusty outflow winds

and brief downpours will be the main threats with any storms. A 

weak shortwave also looks to ride over top of the ridge through 

Utah today, leading to breezy southwest winds across much of the 

area.



Sunday and Monday...The ridge continues to strengthen over Arizona 

through the start of next week, leading to continued dry conditions 

and hot temperatures. Breezier conditions also look to remain as a 

couple week disturbances look to ride overtop of the ridge.



Tuesday through Friday...The ridge looks to gradually shift eastward 

and become centered near New Mexico. Some moisture does look to 

sneak in under the ridge with southerly flow. However, given that 

the ridge is still nearly 597 dam, the odds of being able to convect 

under the ridge seem low. Thus the story looks to remain the same, 

with dry conditions and hot temperatures continuing. The best chance 

for any showers look to mainly be over the higher terrain, but even 

then, this activity would likely be high-based and mainly pose a dry 

lightning and gusty wind threat.



&&



.AVIATION...Saturday 02/18Z through Sunday 03/18Z...Expect mainly 

VFR conditions. ISOLD-SCT -SHRA/-TSRA over southern Apache County 

through 02Z Sunday. Patches MVFR near and north of a KGCN-KPGA line 

due to FU. Daytime sfc winds SW10-20g25-30kts, becoming light 

overnight.



OUTLOOK...Sunday 03/18Z through Tuesday 05/18Z...Expect VFR 

conditions. Patches MVFR near and north of a KGCN-KPGA line due to 

FU. Daytime sfc winds SW10-20g25-30kts each day, becoming light 

overnight. Winds may be slightly stronger on Monday.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...Sunday and Monday...Hot, dry and occasionally windy 

conditions will continue. Daytime minimum relative humidity values 

ranging from 5 to 13 percent each day. Daytime winds southwesterly 

10 to 20 mph, with gusts from 25 to 30 mph Sunday, increasing to 15 

to 25 mph, with gusts as high as 35 mph on Monday.



Tuesday through Thursday...Hot and mostly dry conditions will 

continue. A minor increase in dew points from the south will raise 

minimum relative humidities to around 10 to 20 percent for each day. 

Only slight chances of showers or thunderstorms will develop over 

the higher terrain of central Arizona during this forecast period. 

The Kaiabab Plateau is likely to stay dry. Daytime southwest winds 

10 to 20 mph, with gusts around 25 mph, each day.



&&



.FGZ WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Extreme Heat Warning until 7 PM MST Tuesday for AZZ006.



&&



$$



PUBLIC...Humphreys

AVIATION...Peterson

FIRE WEATHER...Peterson



