Marine Weather HF Voice Broadcast

NWS Ocean Prediction Center Washington DC

1033 AM EDT Sun Aug 3 2025



New England continental shelf and slope waters from 25 nm

offshore to the Hague Line, except to 1000 fm south of New

England.



Conditions given represent the highest within that period.



Seas given as significant wave height, which is the average

height of the highest 1/3 of the waves. Individual waves may be

more than twice the significant wave height.



Locally higher winds and seas can be expected in and near tstms.



.SYNOPSIS FOR NEW ENGLAND WATERS...High pressure will persist

over the area today into Mon while slowly weakening. A weak cold

front will approach from the N by Mon night, then dissipate over

the northern waters Tue as a stronger high builds SE over

northern New England. The high will then dominate most of the

area Tue night through Thu night. Developing low pressure will

pass SE of the waters Mon night into Tue, then pass E of the area

Tue night through Thu night. The National Hurricane Center is

monitoring the low for signs of sub-tropical or tropical cyclone

development and mariners planning voyages to the S or E of the

waters should closely monitor the latest forecast and information

from the National Hurricane Center.  



