FZNT22 KWBC 031434

OFFNT2



Offshore Waters Forecast

NWS Ocean Prediction Center Washington DC

1034 AM EDT Sun Aug 3 2025



West central North Atlantic continental shelf and slope waters

beyond 20 nm to 250 nm offshore, including south of Georges Bank

from 1000 fm to 250 nm offshore.



Seas given as significant wave height, which is the average

height of the highest 1/3 of the waves. Individual waves may be

more than twice the significant wave height.



Locally higher winds and seas can be expected in and near tstms.



ANZ899-040245-

1034 AM EDT Sun Aug 3 2025



.SYNOPSIS FOR MID ATLC WATERS...Developing low pressure located

off the North Carolina coast this morning will track slowly NE 

today along a frontal boundary. A trailing cold front will move 

slowly S over the southern waters into tonight. The low will pass

E of the offshore waters later tonight and Mon with the trailing

cold front stalling over the far S waters. The National 

Hurricane Center is monitoring the low for any signs of sub-

tropical or tropical cyclone development, and mariners should 

monitor the latest forecast and information from the National 

Hurricane Center. Also, all mariners should use caution in or 

near any of the heavier thunderstorms which will likely persist 

mainly near and S of the front and low pressure area today into 

tonight, and also in the strong counterflow mainly in and near 

the W wall of the gulf stream where very gusty winds and very 

rough seas will be likely today. Otherwise, The front will linger

over the far S waters Mon night through Thu night with weak lows

occasionally forming along the frontal boundary. High pressure 

over the far N waters will prevail for the next few days, with a 

stronger high expected to build N of the area Tue night into Thu 

night. 



