=================================================



472

FXUS61 KBUF 031442

AFDBUF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Buffalo NY

1042 AM EDT Sun Aug 3 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

A large area of high pressure will continue to dominate the Great 

Lakes and New England through much of the upcoming week. This will 

support an extended period of dry weather and above normal 

temperatures. While afternoon high temperatures will be well into 

the 80s for lower elevations every day this week, humidity levels 

will remain low to moderate.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH TONIGHT/...

Expansive surface high pressure will continue to dominate the Great 

Lakes and New England today through tonight. Expect nearly full 

sunshine again today, with just a few diurnal cumulus during the 

afternoon along and inland of the lake breeze boundaries. High 

temperatures will be a few degrees warmer than yesterday as the 

airmass continues to modify. Expect highs in the low to mid 80s for 

lower elevations and upper 70s on the hills. Latest HRRR forecast 

continues to suggest vertically integrated smoke and near surface 

smoke will gradually increase today with hazy conditions developing.



Tonight, clear skies continue with typical river valley fog across 

the Southern Tier late.



&&



.SHORT TERM /MONDAY THROUGH TUESDAY NIGHT/...

High pressure remains in control allowing for dry weather across the 

bulk of the area to continue into the first part of next week. The 

primary change will be the position of the high and how that will 

impact temperatures. The high will continue to slowly weaken while 

slowly shifting southeast of our area by Monday. Otherwise, a cold 

front will approach from the north Monday, but is forecast to slow 

southward progression considerably as it runs into the outer 

periphery of the high now centered just to our southeast. This would 

likely keep the southern edge of any widely scattered afternoon 

convection just north of the far North Country during the peak 

heating hours Monday afternoon. What is left of this diffuse 

boundary will continue to very slowly drift south to somewhere near 

the SL Valley/northern NY by Tuesday. Trend in some deterministic 

guidance has been a slight uptick in the chance for some widely 

scattered afternoon convection across the North Country and northern 

NY Tuesday in the vicinity of this diffuse boundary. NBM remains 

dry. With all that said, guidance does advertise a slight uptick in 

low/mid level forcing and moisture near the boundary. At this point 

with the dry antecedent conditions in place, will keep PoPs capped 

just below SChc to show the upward trend, thus keeping the forecast 

dry for now toward the North Country/SL Valley. Will see what future 

trends show over the next model run or two before potentially 

introducing anything higher in the way of PoPs toward the North 

Country/SL Valley Tuesday afternoon.



Otherwise, 850mb temperatures will increase to the mid teens C 

resulting from the weak low-level southerly flow that takes hold on 

the west side of the high as it drifts slowly to our east/southeast. 

This will allow for the slow warming trend to continue through the 

period with daytime highs averaging around 5 degrees above normal 

both days. However, with dew points only rising into the mid and 

upper 50s to low 60s for the bulk of the region, humidity levels 

will remain quite tolerable despite the very warm afternoons. This 

will also keep the overnights more bearable with a fairly sizable 

daily diurnal temperature range.



&&



.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...

Another large area of Canadian high pressure centered over 

central/southern Quebec mid week will build slowly southeast across 

New York and New England through Thursday, before settling just off 

the New England coast by the tail end of the work week into the 

first half of the upcoming weekend...remaining the main feature in 

control of our weather through the period. Meanwhile, whatever is 

left of the lingering diffuse boundary across northern NY should 

finally wash out by mid week. Otherwise, mainly dry weather is 

expected to continue through the period, however due to the 

orientation of the high, a southerly/southeasterly low-level flow of 

Atlantic-based moisture will start to advect in across western and 

north-central NY for the first half of the period. This will allow a 

slight uptick in at least the possibility for some 

isolated/scattered convection during the peak heating hours 

Wednesday and Thursday, primarily targeted along and inland of any 

lake breeze circulations that develop each afternoon. Best chance 

for shower/storm activity over the next seven days looks to be 

Thursday, as a weak mid-level trough crosses over the area. Surface 

high centered off the New England coast then aims to build further 

westward for the latter half of the period, effectively shoving the 

feed of better low-level Atlantic moisture further west out of our 

region.



Otherwise, temperatures will remain fairly consistent through the 

period, with warm daytime highs averaging at least several degrees 

above average. Good news is humidity levels will remain quite 

tolerable through at least the bulk of the period, allowing for a 

decent diurnal temperature range through Friday. This will translate 

to overnight lows averaging in the low and mid 60s across the lower 

elevations, with the much of the higher terrain still able to dip 

down into the mid and upper 50s most nights. Unfortunately, it 

appears both temperatures and humidity levels will then be on the 

rise heading into next weekend, which may bring a return of hot and 

humid conditions.



&&



.AVIATION /15Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Expansive surface high pressure will remain anchored across the 

Great Lakes and New England today through tonight. Expect clear 

skies with a few diurnal cumulus with VFR bases this afternoon. 

Wildfire smoke is forecast to gradually increase today through 

tonight, including near surface smoke. This may eventually bring 

some modest reductions to VSBY. River valley fog will again bring 

local IFR to the Southern Tier late tonight through early Monday.



Outlook...



Monday through Thursday...Mainly VFR. Localized fog and IFR possible 

each late night and early morning across the river valleys of the 

Southern Tier.



&&



.MARINE...

Expansive surface high pressure will remain in control from the 

Great Lakes to New England through much of the week. The light 

synoptic scale flow will generally keep winds and waves minimal 

across most of Lake Erie and Lake Ontario, with lake breeze 

circulations bringing onshore winds each afternoon. 



The lake breeze circulation will combine with some synoptic gradient 

to bring elevated winds and a moderate chop to the northeast end of 

Lake Ontario this afternoon, but winds and waves are expected to 

remain a little below Small Craft Advisory criteria.



&&



.BUF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

NY...Air Quality Alert until midnight EDT tonight for NYZ001>008-

     010>014-019>021-085.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...Hitchcock

NEAR TERM...Hitchcock

SHORT TERM...JM

LONG TERM...JM

AVIATION...Hitchcock

MARINE...Hitchcock



