FZNT24 KWNM 031538

OFFN02



NAVTEX Marine Forecast for Mid-Atlantic states waters

NWS Ocean Prediction Center Washington DC

1138 AM EDT Sun Aug 3 2025



Conditions given represent the highest within that period.



Locally higher winds and seas can be expected in and near tstms.



...Please refer to Coastal Waters Forecasts (CWF) available

through NOAA Weather Radio and other means for detailed coastal

waters forecasts...



.SYNOPSIS...Developing low pressure located off the North

Carolina coast this morning will track slowly NE today along a

frontal boundary. A trailing cold front will move slowly S over

the southern waters into tonight. The low will pass E of the

offshore waters later tonight and Mon with the trailing cold

front stalling over the far S waters. The National Hurricane

Center is monitoring the low for any signs of sub- tropical or

tropical cyclone development, and mariners should monitor the

latest forecast and information from the National Hurricane

Center. Also, all mariners should use caution in or near any of

the heavier thunderstorms which will likely persist mainly near

and S of the front and low pressure area today into tonight, and

also in the strong counterflow mainly in and near the W wall of

the gulf stream where very gusty winds and very rough seas will

be likely today. Otherwise, The front will linger over the far S

waters Mon night through Thu night with weak lows occasionally

forming along the frontal boundary. High pressure over the far N

waters will prevail for the next few days, with a stronger high

expected to build N of the area Tue night into Thu night.  



